<td style=3D"margin:0;background-color:#ffffff;padding:0.05px
2em;min-width:560px;width:560px;" width=3D"560">
<h1 style=3D"text-align: center;"><strong>Who Has Your Voter
File?</strong></h1>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">When you register to vote, you give
the government information about yourself: your name, address, date of
birth and identifying information used to confirm who you are.
Depending on the state, the record may also include a phone number,
email address, party affiliation or voting history.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">That information has become the
subject of a national legal fight. Beginning in 2025, the U.S.
Department of Justice sought unredacted statewide voter-registration
lists from states across the country as part of an effort to identify
people who may be ineligible to vote. Some states provided the
records; others refused, and DOJ sued. On October 9, a federal judge
in Washington, D.C., vacated the Department=E2=80=99s Voter List Maintenanc=
e
Policy, finding that the program exceeded DOJ=E2=80=99s statutory authority
and violated federal privacy and administrative laws.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">So what exactly is in a voter file,
who can see it, and why does the federal government want
it?</span></p>
<p><strong>What Is a Voter File?</strong></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">Every state maintains an official
list of registered voters. These databases allow election officials to
determine whether someone is registered, where that person should
vote, which ballot the voter should receive and whether registration
information needs to be updated.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">The information collected varies by
state, but registration records commonly include a voter=E2=80=99s name,
address, date of birth and some form of identifying number, such as a
driver=E2=80=99s-license number or part of a Social Security number. States
may also collect telephone numbers, email addresses, party affiliation
and other information.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">A voter file may also show voting
history=E2=80=94meaning whether a person participated in particular electio=
ns.
It does not show whom the person voted for. The secret ballot remains
secret.</span></p>
<p><strong>Is Your Voter Information Public?</strong></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">Some of it is. Voter-registration
records are government records, and nearly every state makes at least
some voter information available to certain people or organizations.
The rules vary considerably: some lists are broadly available, while
others are restricted to candidates, political parties, researchers,
journalists, government agencies or organizations engaged in
election-related activity.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">But there is an important
difference between a public voter list and the complete information in
a state=E2=80=99s voter-registration database. Sensitive information=E2=80=
=94such as
Social Security numbers and driver=E2=80=99s-license numbers=E2=80=94is com=
monly
protected from public disclosure. Many states also shield additional
information for voters in address-confidentiality programs, including
victims of domestic violence, stalking and similar crimes.</span></p>
<p><strong>Why Did the Justice Department Want the Lists?</strong></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">DOJ said it needed the records to
enforce federal election laws, evaluate whether states were properly
maintaining their voter rolls and identify people who might be
registered despite being ineligible to vote. The key dispute was over
the scope of the request: DOJ sought more than the voter lists
generally available to the public, including unredacted statewide
records that, in some states, contained full dates of birth,
driver=E2=80=99s-license information and partial Social Security
numbers.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">DOJ also developed a process for
comparing voter information with federal citizenship and immigration
records, including the federal SAVE database. The Department argued
that federal law authorized both collecting the records and sharing
them with the Department of Homeland Security to identify potentially
ineligible voters. In May, DOJ=E2=80=99s Office of Legal Counsel issued a
formal opinion supporting that interpretation.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">Several states disagreed, arguing
that federal law did not authorize such broad demands and that
providing unredacted records would conflict with state and federal
privacy protections.</span></p>
<p><strong>What Did the Court Decide?</strong></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">On October 9, U.S. District Judge
Sparkle Sooknanan vacated DOJ=E2=80=99s Voter List Maintenance
Policy.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">The ruling did not say that the
federal government can never obtain voter-registration records or
enforce federal election law. Instead, the court concluded that DOJ
had not shown that the statutes it relied upon=E2=80=94including the Civil
Rights Act of 1960, the National Voter Registration Act and the Help
America Vote Act=E2=80=94authorized the nationwide program of collecting an=
d
sharing unredacted statewide voter rolls.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">The court also found Privacy Act
violations, including sharing records without adequate legal authority
and failing to make reasonable efforts to ensure the accuracy of
citizenship information before using it in a process that could affect
a person=E2=80=99s voter registration. The record included U.S. citizens wh=
o
had been incorrectly identified and whose registrations were canceled
or questioned.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">Because the court resolved the case
under federal statutes and administrative law, it did not decide the
broader constitutional questions about federal and state authority
over voter registration. The order is appealable.</span></p>
<p><strong>Is the Fight Over?</strong></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">No. The October 9 ruling set aside
the particular DOJ policy challenged in that case, but disputes over
voter eligibility, citizenship verification and federal access to
voter records continue. On October 15, DOJ sued California, asking a
federal court to prevent the counting of mail ballots from more than
20,000 people the Department says may be noncitizens unless their
citizenship is confirmed. California officials dispute the federal
assertions and have questioned the accuracy of the
information.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">The legal debate is not over
whether voter rolls should be accurate. Federal law requires states to
maintain accurate registration lists. The continuing questions are who
may obtain voter information, what information may be shared, which
databases may be used to evaluate eligibility, and what safeguards
must exist before a voter is removed.</span></p>
<p><strong>What Should Voters Know?</strong></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">Voter-registration records are
partly public and partly confidential, and the rules vary by state.
What is public, what remains confidential and who may obtain the
information depend largely on state law.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">The current litigation raises a
broader question=E2=80=94when voters give personal information to the
government so they can exercise the right to vote, who may obtain it,
for what purpose, and under what safeguards?</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"font-weight: 400;">Accurate voter rolls matter, as
does the security of the information used to maintain them=E2=80=94both are
part of protecting the integrity of the vote. =E2=9A=96=EF=B8=8F</span></p>
</td>
